lican-leaning column.", "watch": "State polling once the nominees settle, or a strong Democratic national environment, could tighten the race; continued Republican momentum would push it toward Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Missouri has settled into solid Republican territory at the presidential level, with a partisan lean of roughly nine points Republican after recent cycles. The 2028 race is an open contest: a term-limited incumbent leaves no sitting president on the ballot and no incumbency advantage for either side.\n\nNo public polls of the general election, expert ratings, or candidate fundraising figures are available, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan balance. Headlines about the 2028 Democratic primary calendar and Senator Josh Hawley's decision to rule out a White House bid speak more to nomination politics than to the general election here. Missouri stays in the Republican-leaning column, with wide uncertainty about the eventual margin given the lack of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1545, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2074, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1871, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1883, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2013, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.183, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2061, "margin": -9.0}]}